1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:10,873
Japan, the 1860s. For over 250 years, the Tōkaidō's stations have handed loads and travellers on, station to station.

2
00:00:12,573 --> 00:00:19,313
The road was kept by adjusting it: thirty-six horses at each station, then a hundred, and a hundred porters.

3
00:00:21,013 --> 00:00:26,440
When that wasn't enough, nearby villages had to send people and horses: the helper villages.

4
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:32,173
But travel kept growing, and lords and nobles took horses without paying.

5
00:00:33,673 --> 00:00:39,760
Helper villages were called from further away, and farmers left their fields to carry other people's loads.

6
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:46,204
Their duty grew to several hundred times what was set in 1694.

7
00:00:47,904 --> 00:00:55,395
In 1764, on the Nakasendō, more than two hundred thousand farmers rose against a new demand to carry.

8
00:00:57,295 --> 00:01:03,395
In the 1860s, armies on the march took more than the stations could give, and their money ran out.

9
00:01:05,095 --> 00:01:10,847
The relay couldn't be stretched any further, and the country needed to move more, faster, than horses could.

10
00:01:12,947 --> 00:01:20,518
In 1872, the new government ended the relay and freed the helper villages, and Japan's first railway opened.

11
00:01:22,418 --> 00:01:32,707
The main line west was to cross the mountains. They proved far harder than expected, so in 1886, the line to Nagoya moved to the Tōkaidō's route.

12
00:01:34,607 --> 00:01:44,768
On the first of July 1889, the whole line opened: Tokyo to Kobe in about twenty hours, where Kyoto alone had taken two weeks on foot.

13
00:01:46,868 --> 00:01:53,712
At the utility, a tool's maker is ending its support, with twelve years of the utility's storms locked inside.

14
00:01:55,212 --> 00:02:00,344
So Ravi builds so that any part can change, and what's the utility's own goes with it.

15
00:02:06,844 --> 00:02:11,140
In January, the storm tool's maker writes: support ends in December.

16
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:18,762
The control room bought it twelve years ago, to record every storm, each fault and each line that failed.

17
00:02:20,262 --> 00:02:23,894
All twelve years are inside it, in a format only it can read.

18
00:02:25,594 --> 00:02:30,296
Its one way out is an export made for the regulator's measure, carrying little else.

19
00:02:31,796 --> 00:02:36,029
And Grace's engineers choose which lines to strengthen from those storms.

20
00:02:37,529 --> 00:02:40,522
Without them, next year's work would be chosen blind.

21
00:02:42,422 --> 00:02:47,012
Only the maker can bring it all out, as a project of its own, at its price.

22
00:02:48,712 --> 00:02:54,748
Nobody did anything wrong. But nobody asked, when it was bought, how the utility's data would come out.

23
00:02:56,648 --> 00:03:02,524
So Ravi asks what else holds the utility like this, the data platform's own product included.

24
00:03:04,824 --> 00:03:07,546
Ravi and Tomás start with build or buy.

25
00:03:08,846 --> 00:03:13,794
Buy what every utility needs in much the same way, such as a tool to record storms.

26
00:03:15,494 --> 00:03:21,852
Build what's the utility's own: its rules, its measures, and data products such as hosting capacity.

27
00:03:23,552 --> 00:03:29,001
And before buying, ask how the data comes out, in what format, and how long leaving would take.

28
00:03:30,901 --> 00:03:36,960
The control room's new tool sends every record to the data platform each night, in an open format.

29
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:42,276
Its agreement says the utility may take all of its data, any day.

30
00:03:43,976 --> 00:03:49,225
The data platform is the same: its product bought, what's built on it the utility's own.

31
00:03:51,525 --> 00:03:58,255
Then Ravi looks for seams: places where two parts meet through something both sides agree on, such as a format.

32
00:03:59,955 --> 00:04:04,483
Behind a seam, a part can be replaced, and nothing in front of it has to change.

33
00:04:06,383 --> 00:04:12,774
On the data platform, the tables are files in an open table format, in the utility's own storage.

34
00:04:14,274 --> 00:04:16,994
Any engine that knows the format can read them.

35
00:04:18,694 --> 00:04:22,168
The logic is code in the utility's own repository.

36
00:04:23,668 --> 00:04:28,782
Not everything has a seam: the schedules, alerts and dashboards are the product's own.

37
00:04:30,482 --> 00:04:34,709
That's lock-in, and it isn't wrong: it's the price of what a product does well.

38
00:04:36,409 --> 00:04:40,437
Ravi writes down what leaving would cost, so it's chosen, not found.

39
00:04:42,337 --> 00:04:48,428
The railway took much of the road's route, between the same cities. What changed was what carried the loads.

40
00:04:50,728 --> 00:04:57,340
Next, every technology on Tomás's map gets a stage: tried, in use, on its way out, retired.

41
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:02,965
And a date to look at it again, beside its maker's end of support.

42
00:05:04,465 --> 00:05:09,554
The storm tool would have shown on its way out four years ago, when its maker stopped improving it.

43
00:05:11,454 --> 00:05:16,775
Two more parts turn out to be near their end: a file transfer server and a reporting tool.

44
00:05:18,475 --> 00:05:21,435
Each gets its plan now, years ahead, not months.

45
00:05:23,735 --> 00:05:31,288
Last, an exit plan for every part, the platform's own product included: how it would leave, how long, and at what cost.

46
00:05:32,988 --> 00:05:37,643
A plan nobody has tried is a guess, so the platform team tries it each year.

47
00:05:39,343 --> 00:05:43,970
This year it's Grace's hosting capacity: how much solar each street can take.

48
00:05:45,470 --> 00:05:50,213
For a week, in test, it runs on another engine that reads the same files.

49
00:05:51,913 --> 00:05:55,740
Its models run with eleven lines changed, of fourteen hundred.

50
00:05:57,440 --> 00:06:02,102
The contract, the owner and the glossary don't change, and every number matches.

51
00:06:03,802 --> 00:06:08,551
Three things have to be set up again: its schedule, its alerts, and who may read it.

52
00:06:10,251 --> 00:06:16,638
From that, Ravi works out the whole platform: about eight months to leave, a migration, not a rewrite.

53
00:06:18,738 --> 00:06:22,544
Nobody plans to leave, but now the utility knows what it would take.

54
00:06:24,844 --> 00:06:28,336
Grace asks, in a ticket, for the storm history to be kept.

55
00:06:29,836 --> 00:06:35,662
The maker brings all twelve years out, and the data team checks each against the old tool's reports.

56
00:06:37,362 --> 00:06:43,494
They land on the data platform as files in an open format: storm history, owned by network.

57
00:06:45,194 --> 00:06:48,892
From March, the new tool sends each night's records the same way.

58
00:06:50,392 --> 00:06:54,002
Grace's engineers check them in test, and her ticket closes.

59
00:06:55,502 --> 00:06:58,628
In November, a month early, the old tool is switched off.

60
00:07:00,328 --> 00:07:03,290
Next year's work is chosen from all twelve winters.

61
00:07:05,590 --> 00:07:11,758
Here's the whole data practice on Tomás's map: one data product, from its owner to its files.

62
00:07:13,258 --> 00:07:16,373
In the business layer, Grace holds the data owner's role.

63
00:07:17,673 --> 00:07:22,245
Hosting capacity is a product: it bundles its service and its contract.

64
00:07:23,745 --> 00:07:27,137
What it means is a business object, defined in the glossary.

65
00:07:28,637 --> 00:07:34,150
In the information layer, the data object that realises it: capacity, street by street.

66
00:07:35,650 --> 00:07:41,804
In the application layer, its pipeline and models: one application component, owned by network.

67
00:07:43,304 --> 00:07:47,614
Its output port, an application interface, is where the website reads it.

68
00:07:49,114 --> 00:07:54,999
In the technology layer, the platform's services, which Databricks realises, as system software.

69
00:07:56,499 --> 00:08:04,208
And its artifacts: the tables, as files in an open format, and the models, as code in the utility's repository.

70
00:08:05,908 --> 00:08:13,947
The seam runs between those files and the product that reads them. Another product could read the same files and run the same code.

71
00:08:16,047 --> 00:08:20,287
Ask what breaks if a table changes, and the lines lead up to Grace.

72
00:08:22,187 --> 00:08:28,761
Business architecture for data drew its top, Data architecture its middle, and this series its foundations.

73
00:08:31,061 --> 00:08:39,009
The Gokaidō lasted more than 250 years because it was kept: horses added, villages called on, rules rewritten.

74
00:08:40,709 --> 00:08:46,799
But it never changed who carried the cost. The stations and helper villages bore it, until it broke them.

75
00:08:48,699 --> 00:08:51,931
And when the railway came, many station towns declined.

76
00:08:53,631 --> 00:08:59,289
A data practice isn't built once either: products end, needs grow, and people move on.

77
00:09:00,789 --> 00:09:08,047
So it's kept: every part with a stage and a date, a way out tested each year, and the map changed with every change.

78
00:09:09,747 --> 00:09:15,293
At the utility, the next end is planned, not forced, and what's the utility's own goes with it.

79
00:09:16,993 --> 00:09:22,647
Next, the last series on enterprise architecture: AI and continuous architecture.
